WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.948 --> 00:00:15.948
Hello.

00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:16.949
My name is Ruslan Medzhitov.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:21.354
I'm a professor at Yale University School of Medicine and an Investigator of the

00:00:21.355 --> 00:00:23.389
Howard Hughes Medical Institute.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:32.732
And I will discuss, today, our work on characterizing toll-like receptors and their role in control

00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:36.903
of adaptive immunity.

00:00:36.904 --> 00:00:40.206
My story started in this building.

00:00:40.207 --> 00:00:47.647
This is a Library for Natural Sciences of the Russian Academy of Sciences in Moscow

00:00:47.648 --> 00:00:53.219
when I was a graduate student there from 1990 to '93.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:59.225
At that time, the Soviet Union just collapsed and there was a profound economic crisis,

00:00:59.226 --> 00:01:04.463
so there were no reagents to... to be able to do experimental work, and there were

00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:07.767
not even periodicals available to read.

00:01:07.768 --> 00:01:14.106
And this was the only library that still had a subscription to Nature, Science, Cell,

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:15.741
and other journals.

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:22.315
And so I would go there every morning and spend all day in this library on the second floor, and...

00:01:22.316 --> 00:01:28.187
just trying to read the literature, since I wasn't really able to do much in the lab

00:01:28.188 --> 00:01:30.723
at the time.

00:01:30.724 --> 00:01:37.763
And during one of these trips to the library, I completely randomly ran into a paper written

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:46.772
by the late Charles Janeway that changed the direction of my career and life.

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:53.713
And this was a paper in... that appeared in the Proceedings of the Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory

00:01:53.714 --> 00:01:57.116
from 1989.

00:01:57.117 --> 00:01:59.118
And this is the paper.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:03.789
It was entitled "Approaching the Asymptote? Evolution and Revolution in Immunology".

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:09.729
And this is Charlie Janeway, who unfortunately passed away in 2003.

00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:16.369
But what he described in that paper was a new perspective, a new theory to think about

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:23.676
the immune system that, when I read it, I thought that this is... makes so much sense

00:02:23.677 --> 00:02:29.682
and this is going to completely revolutionize our understanding of immunity.

00:02:29.683 --> 00:02:39.659
And I got completely enchanted with this idea and this logical framework that Charlie proposed there.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:46.499
And that was the time when I became interested in immunology.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:54.740
And to put this into historical context, what the idea was about is how the immune system

00:02:54.741 --> 00:03:02.815
knows when to be activated and when to respond to a challenge.

00:03:02.816 --> 00:03:11.524
And what was thought at the time is that the immune system -- T cells and B cells --

00:03:11.525 --> 00:03:16.028
only react to foreign antigens but not to self-antigens, for example,

00:03:16.029 --> 00:03:19.065
antigens that come from our own tissues.

00:03:19.066 --> 00:03:26.906
And so on the top panel, you'll see here a situation where self-antigens that may be

00:03:26.907 --> 00:03:32.812
presented by antigen-presenting cells -- so, APCs -- to T cells, but there would be

00:03:32.813 --> 00:03:34.280
no response.

00:03:34.281 --> 00:03:40.453
Even though T cells can see the antigen, they should not be responding.

00:03:40.454 --> 00:03:44.624
And at the lower part, you see the situation where there is an infection.

00:03:44.625 --> 00:03:48.828
And when APCs detect pathogens, then there should be response.

00:03:48.829 --> 00:03:52.698
As we all know, we respond to pathogens.

00:03:52.699 --> 00:03:54.634
But it wasn't clear what makes the difference.

00:03:54.635 --> 00:04:01.407
And again, one idea was that the immune system distinguished between self-antigens and non-self-antigens.

00:04:01.408 --> 00:04:07.847
So, pathogens would be non-self, the immune system will react; self-antigens are self,

00:04:07.848 --> 00:04:10.716
and the immune system would not react.

00:04:10.717 --> 00:04:18.558
But it... there was also known at the time that for T cells to become activated

00:04:18.559 --> 00:04:20.159
they require two signals.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:26.599
And one signal is the antigen itself, which is presented by MHC molecules,

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:29.502
major histocompatibility complex molecules.

00:04:29.503 --> 00:04:35.641
So, it's a complex between MHC and antigen that is seen by T cells.

00:04:35.642 --> 00:04:42.748
And what became known at the time, from the work Ron Schwarz and Mark Jenkins at the NIH,

00:04:42.749 --> 00:04:49.922
is that when T cells see only signal 1, there is no response that is generated,

00:04:49.923 --> 00:04:54.727
but when they see two signals -- the second signal also coming from antigen-presenting cells --

00:04:54.728 --> 00:04:57.396
then there would be a response.

00:04:57.397 --> 00:05:03.035
And the identity of that second signal was subsequently characterized, and it's

00:05:03.036 --> 00:05:09.876
known as molecules called B7-1/B7-2, or CD80/CD86.

00:05:09.877 --> 00:05:18.384
So, that was the state of knowledge, and it was... many different views about what controls

00:05:18.385 --> 00:05:22.822
activation of the adaptive immune response, of T cells and B cells.

00:05:22.823 --> 00:05:28.327
And there were different sorts of schools of thought and... and it was still very much

00:05:28.328 --> 00:05:34.734
unclear, for somebody who's coming from the outside of the field, what was going on and

00:05:34.735 --> 00:05:36.869
how the system worked.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:43.075
And that's what was changed with this article by Charlie Janeway, where he proposed that,

00:05:43.076 --> 00:05:48.648
in fact, what might be happening is that in addition to T cell receptors and immunoglobulin receptors

00:05:48.649 --> 00:05:56.055
there is a whole different set of receptors, that are shown here in blue,

00:05:56.056 --> 00:06:04.230
that Charlie hypothesized would be involved in direct detection of pathogens, and that

00:06:04.231 --> 00:06:09.268
these receptors would then control expression of the signal 2.

00:06:09.269 --> 00:06:15.041
And in this manner, when antigen-presenting cells encounter self-antigens or any other

00:06:15.042 --> 00:06:21.113
antigens that are not infectious, there would be just signal 1 and there will be no response.

00:06:21.114 --> 00:06:26.252
But when they encounter pathogens, then there would be this detection, by these hypothetical receptors,

00:06:26.253 --> 00:06:33.426
of common microbial structures and that would lead to the induction of signal 2,

00:06:33.427 --> 00:06:35.995
and that would result in the immune response.

00:06:35.996 --> 00:06:42.568
And that was a very profound insight and it was a very beautiful idea that made so much sense.

00:06:42.569 --> 00:06:48.608
And... and Charlie further proposed that these receptors, that he called pattern-recognition receptors,

00:06:48.609 --> 00:06:54.547
because they detect conserved patterns found in pathogens... things like lipopolysaccharides,

00:06:54.548 --> 00:07:00.253
peptidoglycans, and so forth... he proposed that these receptors are evolutionary ancient

00:07:00.254 --> 00:07:08.094
and they are involved in immunity in invertebrates as well as in vertebrates, where they control

00:07:08.095 --> 00:07:11.130
the innate arm of the immune system.

00:07:11.131 --> 00:07:15.468
But in addition, in vertebrates they acquire a second function where they control activation

00:07:15.469 --> 00:07:17.069
of the adaptive immune system.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:22.475
And that was a really revolutionary proposition.

00:07:22.476 --> 00:07:29.615
And the only problem was that the receptors that Charlie hypothesized should exist were

00:07:29.616 --> 00:07:31.450
not known at the time.

00:07:31.451 --> 00:07:36.756
And... and so, after reading Charlie's paper, I started communicating with him,

00:07:36.757 --> 00:07:42.728
initially through email, and eventually I was fortunate enough to be able to join his lab as a...

00:07:42.729 --> 00:07:45.898
as a postdoc in 1994.

00:07:45.899 --> 00:07:52.572
And then my focus of my research was on trying to identify such receptors that can

00:07:52.573 --> 00:07:58.945
control expression of signal 2 to induce... to control activation of adaptive immunity.

00:07:58.946 --> 00:08:05.451
And at that time, there was really little known about how microbes can be recognized directly.

00:08:05.452 --> 00:08:08.788
There were a few proteins that were known to bind to microbial cell walls.

00:08:08.789 --> 00:08:14.026
One of them was a protein called mannan-binding lectin from the complement system.

00:08:14.027 --> 00:08:19.632
Another is CD14 molecule, which is a GPI-anchored protein on macrophages.

00:08:19.633 --> 00:08:27.673
And there were a few proteins known in invertebrates that were involved in recognition of LPS.

00:08:27.674 --> 00:08:31.544
But other than that, it was really not clear what these receptors are supposed to

00:08:31.545 --> 00:08:37.183
look like and how they... how to identify them.

00:08:37.184 --> 00:08:43.689
And a couple of things that changed, that played a role in the development of the story,

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:48.661
was that... one was that in 1994, the year when I joined Charlie's lab, David Baltimore

00:08:48.662 --> 00:08:55.668
came to Yale to give a talk in our department, and at that time he just... his lab just started

00:08:55.669 --> 00:09:00.806
characterizing the first knockouts for NF-kappaB, which is a critical transcription factor

00:09:00.807 --> 00:09:03.342
involved in inflammation.

00:09:03.343 --> 00:09:08.481
And there he talked about the phenotype of the first NF-kappaB knockouts, which had

00:09:08.482 --> 00:09:12.818
both defects in innate and in adaptive immune response.

00:09:12.819 --> 00:09:19.458
And I remember, after David's talk, Charlie and I were talking in the hallway,

00:09:19.459 --> 00:09:25.298
and we both thought that whatever it is that we're looking for, it's... whatever these receptors are,

00:09:25.299 --> 00:09:30.703
they probably work by activating NF-kappaB.

00:09:30.704 --> 00:09:34.473
And then we thought, okay, so one criteria we should use is that they... they are likely

00:09:34.474 --> 00:09:37.210
to be NF-kappaB activators.

00:09:37.211 --> 00:09:43.716
And at that time, the only two receptor families known to activate NF-kappaB where the TNF family

00:09:43.717 --> 00:09:47.019
and IL-1 receptor family.

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:58.164
And IL-1 receptor is the one that we got particularly interested in because of this remarkable conservation

00:09:58.165 --> 00:10:06.272
in IL-1 receptor signaling portion with a receptor that at the time was already known

00:10:06.273 --> 00:10:09.275
to exist in Drosophila.

00:10:09.276 --> 00:10:11.677
And in Drosophila that receptor is called Toll.

00:10:11.678 --> 00:10:19.018
That receptor was identified and cloned in Kathryn Anderson's lab by Carl Hashimoto.

00:10:19.019 --> 00:10:21.387
At the time, they were at Berkeley.

00:10:21.388 --> 00:10:28.127
And the signaling pathway was elucidated by several investigators, including Steve Wasserman

00:10:28.128 --> 00:10:32.365
and Mike Levine, Kathryn Anderson and others.

00:10:32.366 --> 00:10:37.537
And it was known that this pathway operates in fly development.

00:10:37.538 --> 00:10:42.542
But what was interesting for us is that it worked through NF-kappaB in flies, and the

00:10:42.543 --> 00:10:47.613
related receptor, IL-1 receptor, worked through NF-kappaB in mammals.

00:10:47.614 --> 00:10:54.487
And what they shared is the cytoplasmic portion, shown here in blue, whereas their ectodomains,

00:10:54.488 --> 00:10:59.258
involved in ligand recognition, were unrelated.

00:10:59.259 --> 00:11:09.335
And because of this conservation with flies, and because IL-1 receptor is involved in inflammation,

00:11:09.336 --> 00:11:15.107
we thought that perhaps the receptor that we are looking for would be... would have

00:11:15.108 --> 00:11:21.214
this C-terminal signaling domain, and the ectodomain perhaps would be something that

00:11:21.215 --> 00:11:22.882
can recognize microbes.

00:11:22.883 --> 00:11:28.688
And the only structures that were known at the time to recognize microbial components

00:11:28.689 --> 00:11:30.756
were C-type lectins.

00:11:30.757 --> 00:11:35.528
So I thought that maybe there is another version of this receptor that has cytoplasmic signaling domain

00:11:35.529 --> 00:11:40.299
from Toll and IL-1 receptor, and the ectodomain would be from C-type lectin.

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:44.971
And... and that was my strategy to try to identify it.

00:11:44.972 --> 00:11:52.078
And... that... what I took advantage of is that, after many, many failed attempts

00:11:52.079 --> 00:12:00.720
to do it through various types of approaches... degenerate screening or by hybridization or

00:12:00.721 --> 00:12:07.627
through PCR... took advantage of the fact that, at the time, new types of genomic sequences

00:12:07.628 --> 00:12:13.332
started to be become available -- these so-called expressed sequence tags -- that were

00:12:13.333 --> 00:12:17.737
just short sequences that were generated randomly in multiple tissues.

00:12:17.738 --> 00:12:23.876
And using bioinformatics approaches and using conserved consensus sequences for the cytoplasmic domain

00:12:23.877 --> 00:12:29.148
for Toll and IL-1 receptor, as well as for C-type lectins, I started searching

00:12:29.149 --> 00:12:35.421
these databases and found several clones that corresponded either to C-type lectins or to

00:12:35.422 --> 00:12:37.723
this cytoplasmic domain.

00:12:37.724 --> 00:12:40.092
And then I pursued both of them.

00:12:40.093 --> 00:12:46.532
And the one that corresponded to C-type lectin domain, I found that it was most similar to

00:12:46.533 --> 00:12:48.768
Drosophila Toll.

00:12:48.769 --> 00:12:55.942
And this was in some... starting in January, February in 1996.

00:12:55.943 --> 00:13:06.786
And the... the gel shown on the left here was one of the first clones that I got,

00:13:06.787 --> 00:13:11.490
which was... later would turn out to be a human homologue of Toll.

00:13:11.491 --> 00:13:15.561
And the gel on the right in this bottom band...

00:13:15.562 --> 00:13:21.167
I still remember that band because this was one of those happy moments in the lab

00:13:21.168 --> 00:13:27.306
when I got this portion of the receptor, which was particularly difficult to clone.

00:13:27.307 --> 00:13:34.814
And then by doing standard library screening through hybridization, we pulled out a full-length

00:13:34.815 --> 00:13:44.724
of the cloning... coding Toll receptor followed by 5' RACE technique, which was at the time

00:13:44.725 --> 00:13:50.730
popular in gene cloning, and eventually got the receptor.

00:13:50.731 --> 00:13:54.634
And... by about May of '96.

00:13:54.635 --> 00:14:00.673
And then another event that happened that... in the... in the summer of '96, we had a meeting

00:14:00.674 --> 00:14:06.279
in Charlie's summer house in Annisquam, near Boston, where several other investigators

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:12.985
participated that shared Human Frontier Grant, including Alan Ezekowitz, Fotis Kafatos,

00:14:12.986 --> 00:14:15.221
Jules Hoffmann, and Charlie.

00:14:15.222 --> 00:14:20.426
And... and Jules Hoffmann then presented work of Bruno Lemaitre from his lab about

00:14:20.427 --> 00:14:25.031
genetic work on Drosophila Toll pathway, where they found it was involved in immunity.

00:14:25.032 --> 00:14:34.140
So, that was... it was further confirmation for our expectation that this receptor

00:14:34.141 --> 00:14:36.275
might be involved in immunity.

00:14:36.276 --> 00:14:46.752
And so, then I characterized this receptor in terms of its ability to control NF-kappaB signaling,

00:14:46.753 --> 00:14:49.322
shown here on the right.

00:14:49.323 --> 00:14:54.694
And more importantly for us at the time, for its ability to induce inflammatory cytokines.

00:14:54.695 --> 00:14:59.098
And the most important for us, the holy grail for us, whether this receptor can induce

00:14:59.099 --> 00:15:04.337
the second signal, or costimulatory molecule, a channel known as B7.

00:15:04.338 --> 00:15:10.009
And that was the... the probably... one of those lucky moments in the lab when I saw

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:16.782
that Toll receptor, which I rendered constitutively active because the ligand was not yet known

00:15:16.783 --> 00:15:22.121
at the time, and transfected in this monocyte cell line.

00:15:22.122 --> 00:15:27.293
And when I saw that it can induce expression of this gene, that was the moment when

00:15:27.294 --> 00:15:32.098
we thought, okay, this... this whole hypothesis, now, we can connect from the beginning to

00:15:32.099 --> 00:15:33.366
the end.

00:15:33.367 --> 00:15:37.103
And I even called Charlie... this was late in the evening, I even called Charlie and

00:15:37.104 --> 00:15:40.506
told him that that human Toll can induce B7 expression.

00:15:40.507 --> 00:15:44.477
That was... we probably would be the only two people in the world who would be

00:15:44.478 --> 00:15:50.116
excited about that, but nevertheless others also appreciated it.

00:15:50.117 --> 00:15:55.288
And so we published this paper in '97, where... which is an extremely simple paper showing

00:15:55.289 --> 00:15:59.959
just cloning, and showing these couple of functional assays.

00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:07.834
And... and then, subsequently, in the same year, Ben Lewin, who was the editor of Cell at the time,

00:16:07.835 --> 00:16:12.438
asked Charlie to write a mini-review about innate immunity.

00:16:12.439 --> 00:16:23.149
And so in that mini-review we discussed questions about evolution of innate and adaptive immunity,

00:16:23.150 --> 00:16:28.788
and put this schematic together to... to show the parallels and differences between

00:16:28.789 --> 00:16:37.430
Drosophila Toll and mammalian Toll pathways, where we suggested that recognition of pathogen-associated

00:16:37.431 --> 00:16:44.070
molecular patterns -- things like LPS and teichoic acids and so on -- is detected by Tolls,

00:16:44.071 --> 00:16:49.175
either directly or through some intermediary, as is the case in Drosophila.

00:16:49.176 --> 00:16:54.247
And that leads to induction of NF-kappaB and various innate immune response and genes

00:16:54.248 --> 00:16:57.149
genes controlling adaptive immunity.

00:16:57.150 --> 00:17:06.726
And subsequent work indeed demonstrated that the Toll that I worked on is part of a bigger family.

00:17:06.727 --> 00:17:13.099
There are about a dozen different Toll receptors in mammalian species.

00:17:13.100 --> 00:17:18.171
And it's now known as TLR4 -- Toll-like receptor 4 -- and it's a receptor for LPS, and the

00:17:18.172 --> 00:17:25.278
receptor part that detects LPS... actually, a protein called MG2 that complexes with Toll.

00:17:25.279 --> 00:17:32.785
And subsequent work with... by many investigators elucidated specificities of different

00:17:32.786 --> 00:17:33.786
Toll receptors.

00:17:33.787 --> 00:17:39.358
And Shizuo Akira from Osaka University, in particular, played a major role in this

00:17:39.359 --> 00:17:42.061
set of investigations.

00:17:42.062 --> 00:17:50.503
And what we know now is that... if we... our main interest was not so much in microbial

00:17:50.504 --> 00:17:56.075
specificity of receptors but in this idea that microbial receptors can control activation

00:17:56.076 --> 00:17:58.277
of adaptive immunity.

00:17:58.278 --> 00:18:00.646
And this is what is schematically illustrated here.

00:18:00.647 --> 00:18:07.386
So, we have a dendritic cell, which is a cell type that normally activates T cells.

00:18:07.387 --> 00:18:11.757
And when dendritic cell encounters pathogens, what's shown on the left side, here,

00:18:11.758 --> 00:18:16.529
there is an endocytic receptor that will take up pathogen, take it into lysosomes, and then

00:18:16.530 --> 00:18:23.603
proteins will be cut into peptides and loaded into MHC molecules and presented to

00:18:23.604 --> 00:18:25.705
T cell receptors.

00:18:25.706 --> 00:18:30.376
But that information by itself is not sufficient for T cells to know whether they should become

00:18:30.377 --> 00:18:37.850
activated or not, because this peptide can come from pathogens or it can come from innocuous

00:18:37.851 --> 00:18:40.720
food antigens or it could be even a self-antigen.

00:18:40.721 --> 00:18:42.655
So, T cells have no way...

00:18:42.656 --> 00:18:46.726
no way of knowing what the origin of the antigen is.

00:18:46.727 --> 00:18:50.429
And the reason for that is because T cell receptors are generated at random.

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:56.402
And each T cell has one single specificity, but they are random, so it doesn't know

00:18:56.403 --> 00:18:58.538
what it's specific for.

00:18:58.539 --> 00:19:04.243
So therefore, there is a need for another signal, so-called signal 2, which will provide

00:19:04.244 --> 00:19:09.448
information about the origin of the antigen that T cell is specific for.

00:19:09.449 --> 00:19:14.787
And that is provided by this detection of microbial structures by Toll receptors

00:19:14.788 --> 00:19:18.791
-- and now we know, several other families of pattern-recognition receptors --

00:19:18.792 --> 00:19:23.462
that detect those structures and induce expression of costimulatory molecules.

00:19:23.463 --> 00:19:28.901
And now a T cell that has specificity for peptide derived from pathogen will also sec...

00:19:28.902 --> 00:19:38.110
have a confirmation signal from a costimulatory molecule -- CD80/CD86, also known as B7-1/B7-2 --

00:19:38.111 --> 00:19:40.513
and then the T cell will become activated.

00:19:40.514 --> 00:19:45.084
And in addition, Toll receptors and other pattern-recognition receptors will

00:19:45.085 --> 00:19:51.357
induce production of cytokines such as IL-12 that will tell T cells what kind of effector response

00:19:51.358 --> 00:19:52.692
to generate.

00:19:52.693 --> 00:20:03.669
And that is now, of course, a well-accepted view of how immune response is activated.

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:11.744
And... and it's a... it's very satisfying to see this confirmation of the proposal

00:20:11.745 --> 00:20:16.115
by Charlie Janeway, which was at the time largely ignored.

00:20:16.116 --> 00:20:22.855
And that now it's become... it's a... it's a textbook knowledge in the... of the immune system,

00:20:22.856 --> 00:20:27.527
that that's how activation of the adaptive immune system is controlled.

00:20:27.528 --> 00:20:34.600
And Toll receptor was one of the first receptors to be demonstrated to... to be involved

00:20:34.601 --> 00:20:35.601
in this process.

00:20:35.602 --> 00:20:38.838
So, that's the story of this discovery.

00:20:38.839 --> 00:20:57.024
And thank you for listening.

